===BODY===
          <h2>Britain's first full-length motorway</h2>
          <p>The M1 was the first inter-urban motorway in Britain, and it is still one of the busiest. It starts in north-west London near Staples Corner, crosses the M25 at junction 6a near Watford, and runs north past Luton, Milton Keynes and Northampton before meeting the M6 at Catthorpe near Rugby. From there it continues past Leicester, Nottingham and Sheffield and ends at Leeds, where it joins the A1(M). A breakdown on the M1 can happen in very different surroundings, from dense urban traffic at the London end to long rural stretches through the Midlands.</p>

          <h2>London to Northampton (junctions 1–16)</h2>
          <p>The southern end carries heavy commuter traffic into and out of London, and the interchange with the M25 at junction 6a is one of the busiest in the country. North of there the motorway runs past Luton airport (junction 10) and Milton Keynes (junctions 13 and 14) to Northampton. Much of this section is smart motorway without a permanent hard shoulder, so knowing where the next emergency area is can make a real difference if your car starts to struggle.</p>

          <h2>The Midlands (junctions 16–28)</h2>
          <p>Watford Gap services, between junctions 16 and 17, is one of the oldest service areas in Britain and a well-known landmark. Just north of it, the M6 splits away at junction 19. The M1 then passes Leicester (junction 21, where the M69 heads to Coventry), Leicester Forest East services, East Midlands Airport and Donington Park near junction 23a, and Nottingham around junctions 24 to 26. Traffic here mixes long-distance freight with local journeys, and queues build up quickly after an incident.</p>

          <h2>South Yorkshire to Leeds (junctions 28–48)</h2>
          <p>Further north the M1 runs past Chesterfield and into South Yorkshire, crossing the Tinsley Viaduct by Sheffield and Rotherham at junction 34. It then passes Barnsley and Wakefield, meets the M62 at the Lofthouse interchange (junction 42) and continues around the east side of <a href="{{R}}areas/leeds.html">Leeds</a> to its end at the A1(M). Services at Tibshelf, Woodall and Woolley Edge are useful reference points when you call.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>If you break down on the M1:</strong> get as far left as you can, onto the hard shoulder or into an emergency area. Switch your hazard lights on, leave the car by the passenger door if it's safe and wait behind the barrier. If you're stuck in a live lane and can't get out safely, stay in the car with your seatbelt on and call 999. Then call us with the junction numbers either side of you and your direction.</p>
          </div>

          <h2>Smart motorway sections</h2>
          <p>Large parts of the M1, especially between Luton and Sheffield, are smart motorways. On sections where the hard shoulder has become a running lane, emergency areas (orange surface, blue SOS sign) are the safest place to stop. A red X above a lane means it is closed. Read our guide to <a href="{{R}}guides/smart-motorway-breakdown.html">breaking down on a smart motorway</a> before your next long drive.</p>

          <h2>How we recover you</h2>
          <p>We take your location, vehicle and where you want it taken, then send the nearest available local recovery operator. Your vehicle can be taken to a safe place off the motorway, home, or straight to a garage of your choice, and we'll agree the price and give you an honest arrival estimate before anyone sets off.</p>
===FAQ===
Q: How do I find my exact location on the M1?
A: Use the blue driver location signs along the verge, which show the motorway, the direction (A or B) and a distance number. The last junction or services you passed and your direction of travel, for example southbound towards London, also help. You can share your live location with us on WhatsApp too.
Q: Can you recover me from the M1 near London as well as further north?
A: Yes. We arrange recovery along the whole M1, from the London end through the Midlands and Yorkshire to Leeds. How quickly someone reaches you depends on where the nearest available operator is, and we'll tell you before we book it.
Q: My car has broken down at a service station on the M1. Is that easier?
A: Usually, yes. A service area is a much safer place to wait than the hard shoulder. Tell us which services you're at and roughly where in the car park you are, and we'll arrange recovery from there.
